### `EtFormErrorMessage`

Aggregates errors from **multiple fields** into a shared display block.
Requires `<FormProvider>` as an ancestor (uses `useFormContext` internally).

**Sub-components:**

| Sub-component | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `EtFormErrorMessage` (root) | Reads errors for listed `names` from `FormProvider` context |
| `EtFormErrorMessage.Text` | Renders errors. `mode="first"` (default) shows only first; `mode="all"` shows all |

**When to use:**
- Multiple controls (e.g., two checkboxes) share one error display block
- You want to suppress inline errors on individual controls and centralise them

**Usage:**

```tsx
<FormProvider {...methods}>
  <EtFormErrorMessage names={['agree', 'notification']}>
    <EtFormCheckbox
      name="agree"
      control={control}
      rules={{ validate: (v) => Boolean(v) || 'You must agree to terms' }}
    >
      <EtFormCheckbox.Control>
        <EtFormCheckbox.Label>I agree to terms</EtFormCheckbox.Label>
      </EtFormCheckbox.Control>
    </EtFormCheckbox>

    <EtFormCheckbox
      name="notification"
      control={control}
      rules={{ validate: (v) => Boolean(v) || 'Enable notifications to proceed' }}
    >
      <EtFormCheckbox.Control>
        <EtFormCheckbox.Label>Enable notifications</EtFormCheckbox.Label>
      </EtFormCheckbox.Control>
    </EtFormCheckbox>

    <EtFormErrorMessage.Text mode="first" />
  </EtFormErrorMessage>
</FormProvider>
```

**Custom render:**

```tsx
<EtFormErrorMessage.Text
  render={({ errors }) => (
    <View>{errors.map((e) => <Text key={e.name}>{e.message}</Text>)}</View>
  )}
/>
```

---

## Choosing Between Inline and Shared Error Display

| Scenario | Pattern |
|---|---|
| Single field with its own error | Use `<EtFormInput.ErrorMessage />` / `<EtFormCheckbox.ErrorMessage />` inline |
| Multiple fields sharing one error block | Use `<EtFormErrorMessage names={[...]}><EtFormErrorMessage.Text /></EtFormErrorMessage>` with `<FormProvider>` |
| Inline control, but error shown elsewhere | Pass `hideError` to `EtFormInput`, omit `ErrorMessage` sub-component on individual controls |

---

## Type-Safe Field Names

All wrappers are generic over your form's value type, so `name` is type-checked:

```tsx
type MyForm = { email: string; age: number };
const { control } = useForm<MyForm>();

<EtFormInput name="email" control={control} />   // ✅ string field
<EtFormInput name="age" control={control} />     // ❌ age is not a string field
<EtFormInput name="phone" control={control} />   // ❌ unknown field
```

